Now I understand the flow. The `GenField<CTYPE>` function calls `GetFieldDefault<T>(fd)` when the field is not present in the table. `GetFieldDefault` calls `StringToNumber(fd.value.constant.c_str(), &val)`.

The key insight is: `fd.value.constant` is the default value string from the schema. If this string is something that `StringToNumber` can't parse, `val` would be set to 0 (for most types). But wait - let me check if there's a case where `StringToNumber` doesn't set `*val`.

Looking at `StringToNumber<int64_t>`:
```cpp
template<> inline bool StringToNumber<int64_t>(const char *str, int64_t *val) {
  return StringToIntegerImpl(val, str);
}
```

And `StringToIntegerImpl` with `check_errno=true`:
```cpp
if (check_errno) errno = 0;
auto endptr = str;
strtoval_impl(val, str, const_cast<char **>(&endptr), base);
if ((*endptr != '\0') || (endptr == str)) {
  *val = 0;
  return false;
}
if (check_errno && errno) return false;
return true;
```

When `errno` is set (out-of-range), it returns `false` without setting `*val`. But `strtoval_impl` already wrote to `*val` (it sets it to MAX/MIN on overflow). So `*val` is initialized.

